  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "To file a federal tax extension and get until October 15 to file, submit Form 4868 by April 15. You can do this for free via IRS Free File, using tax software, or by paying all/part of your taxes owed online IRS Direct Pay and selecting \"Extension\". Note that an extension is to file, not to pay taxes owed.Top Ways to File a Tax ExtensionIRS Free File (Recommended): Use IRS Free File to electronically submit Form 4868 for free, regardless of income.Electronic Payment: Make a partial or full payment via IRS Direct Pay, debit/credit card, or Digital Wallet, and select \"4868\" or \"Extension\" as the reason. This automatically triggers an extension without filing the form separately.Tax Software/Pro: Use tax software (e.g., TurboTax, H&R Block) or a tax professional to e-file.Mail: File a paper Form 4868 by mail. It must be postmarked by April 15.Important ConsiderationsDeadline: The extension request must be submitted by April 15.Payment Requirement: If you owe taxes, you must still pay the estimated amount due by April 15 to avoid penalties and interest.Eligibility: Anyone can file an extension, regardless of income.Confirmation: E-filing provides a confirmation number for your records.State Taxes: A federal extension does not always cover state taxes; check your state's tax agency requirements.",
